tin: a new always-on serving box to spec

cld-strand-astro-serving.md proposes splitting processing (nit) from serving
(tin). tin holds frame sets resident and serves browsers: modest CPU, quiet,
low-power, always on — a different machine character from nit, and a build this
strand owns. The doc's own caveat: the NAS build may answer the storage-topology
question (does tin hold a copy, or mount from nit / the NAS?) and make tin much
thinner than currently imagined. So do NOT buy for tin before the NAS decision —
spec it after.
